  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pon-04-consecutive-patterned-numbers",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Consecutive and Patterned Numbers",
    "description": "Model consecutive integers, odd/even runs and consecutive multiples with the correct step size, then exploit their symmetry.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the lesson around symmetry: odd-sized equally spaced lists balance around one middle value, while even-sized lists balance around a midpoint. Then vary the step size—1, 2 or a fixed multiple—before moving to sums, prime follow-ups, consecutive products, square sums and difference-of-squares capstones. The learner always identifies the spacing before using a shortcut.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "Teach consecutive integers as x,x+1,...; consecutive odd/even integers as x,x+2,...; and consecutive multiples of k using step k. Start with the middle-number shortcut for odd-sized equally spaced lists, then handle even-sized lists, known sums, products, sums of squares and differences of squares. Include follow-up requests such as the largest term, the next block of consecutive odd numbers, or the count of primes after the sequence is found. Emphasize that the shortcut comes from symmetry and only works after the spacing pattern is identified correctly."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's make consecutive-number questions visual in your head.\n\nInstead of treating every term separately, look for the centre and the fixed step. Most of the chapter's 'long' sequences then collapse to one or two numbers.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Consecutive numbers are equally spaced. That symmetry makes the middle number special.\n\nFor 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, pairs around 23 balance:\n21+25 = 46\n22+24 = 46",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "So the average is the middle number, 23.\n\nFor any odd count of consecutive integers, once the spacing is equal, the average is the centre term.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
